      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16{y}^{2}(\\cos{(6 x )})^{2}=(6+(4)y\\cos{(6 x )})^{2}\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\small \\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16{y}^{2}(\\cos{(6 x )})^{2}=(6+(4)y\\cos{(6 x )})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16{y}^{2}(\\cos{(6 x )})^{2}=36+16{y}^{2}(\\cos{(6 x )})^{2}+48 y \\cos{(6 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=36+48 y \\cos{(6 x )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(- 48 \\cos{(6 x )})}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{36}_{Q(x)}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=- 48 \\cos{(6 x )}$ and $Q(x)=36.$",
